LeetCode 622 — Design Circular Queue

From Brute Force → Most Optimal

All approaches + code + explanation + dry run
1. Problem Understanding

A Queue follows:

FIFO = First In First Out

For example:

Front                    Rear
 ↓                         ↓
10 → 20 → 30 → 40

Operations:

enQueue(value) → insert at rear
deQueue() → remove from front
Front() → return front element
Rear() → return rear element
isEmpty()
isFull()

The special requirement is that the queue should be circular.

So when we reach the end of the array:

index 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 0 → 1 ...

Approach 1 — Simple Array + Shifting
Idea

Use a normal array.

For insertion:

[10, 20, 30, _, _]

Add 40:

[10, 20, 30, 40, _]

For deletion from the front, shift everything left:

Before:
[10, 20, 30, 40]


deQueue()


After:
[20, 30, 40, _]
Code
class MyCircularQueue {


    int[] data;
    int size;
    int capacity;


    public MyCircularQueue(int k) {
        capacity = k;
        data = new int[k];
        size = 0;
    }


    public boolean enQueue(int value) {


        if (isFull()) {
            return false;
        }


        data[size] = value;
        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deQueue() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        // Shift elements left
        for (int i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
            data[i] = data[i + 1];
        }


        size--;


        return true;
    }


    public int Front() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return data[0];
    }


    public int Rear() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return data[size - 1];
    }


    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }


    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }
}

Complexity
Operation	Time
enQueue()	O(1)
deQueue()	O(K)
Front()	O(1)
Rear()	O(1)

The problem is:

deQueue() → shifting → O(K)

We want O(1).

