class MyCircularQueue {


    class Node {
        int val;
        Node next;


        Node(int val) {
            this.val = val;
        }
    }


    Node front;
    Node rear;


    int size;
    int capacity;


    public MyCircularQueue(int k) {
        capacity = k;
        size = 0;
    }


    public boolean enQueue(int value) {


        if (isFull()) {
            return false;
        }


        Node node = new Node(value);


        if (isEmpty()) {
            front = rear = node;
        } else {
            rear.next = node;
            rear = node;
        }


        size++;


        return true;
    }


    public boolean deQueue() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }


        front = front.next;


        size--;


        if (size == 0) {
            rear = null;
        }


        return true;
    }


    public int Front() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return front.val;
    }


    public int Rear() {


        if (isEmpty()) {
            return -1;
        }


        return rear.val;
    }


    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }


    public boolean isFull() {
        return size == capacity;
    }
}

Complexity
enQueue  → O(1)
deQueue  → O(1)
Front    → O(1)
Rear     → O(1)


Space → O(K)

This is efficient, but it isn't actually using a circular array, which is the main concept being tested by this problem.

So let's implement the actual circular queue.

Approach 3 — Circular Array ⭐

Instead of shifting elements, we use two pointers:

front
rear

and a size.

The array wraps around using %.

For example, capacity = 5:

0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4
↑               ↓
└───────────────┘

After index 4:

(rear + 1) % capacity

becomes:

(4 + 1) % 5 = 0
